# Attempt 2: Fedora — Review

Omarchy knew exactly what it wanted to be, but it still let users decide how
to present it. Fedora felt much cleaner, and lets the user customize it just
as completely. I really enjoyed Fedora. With Omarchy, I felt overwhelmed,
not even knowing how to open apps at first, but Fedora let me switch over
from Windows much more easily. It was similar enough to feel familiar, but
different enough to stand out, which I really liked.

Using Fedora, I fell in love with Linux and with building projects in
general. For the first time, things like `sudo`, `dnf` (and how it compared
to `apt` or `pacman`), and other core commands actually started making sense
to me, rather than just being instructions I copied without understanding. I
never enjoyed using the terminal much during Omarchy, but Fedora is where
that changed. It made me realise how effective hands-on projects are for
actually understanding what I learned, and how to properly use it. Wanting
to improve, I watched videos and learned through TryHackMe rooms. I became
more comfortable with the terminal and started using it daily, to create
files, move them, update my system, install software, and more, so much so
that I started installing apps through PowerShell using `winget`.

Fedora comes in two main versions: one with KDE Plasma, which gives a more
Windows-like appearance, and one with GNOME, which feels closer to Mac. I
picked KDE, but if you want a different window manager, like Hyprland or
XFCE, you can install and set one up later. As I mentioned, letting users
pick what they wanted from their OS was a huge factor in why I chose Fedora.
It also has a six-month release cycle with frequent kernel updates, so the
system stays up to date with whatever new open-source software gets
released. Although proprietary, it also has solid NVIDIA driver support,
which mattered to me since my laptop has an NVIDIA graphics card.

Overall, it was a wonderful experience, and I learned a lot from it. I'd
definitely recommend Fedora to people wanting to get into Linux. I'd
recommend it over Mint or Ubuntu, since it strikes a great balance between
what makes Linux unique and what Windows users are used to, making it a good
middle ground for newcomers like me.

**Rating: 10/10**
